{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>At that time.</b> After the Holy One, blessed be He, forgave them, He said to him: ״פסל לך״ — lest they say and complain about them, saying: I lost a desirable thing, namely the tablets that you shattered. And thus it is written: ״לכל זמן ועת לכל חפץ״ — there was a time for the tablets to be shattered and a time for others to be made, and this is ״פסל לך״. Another interpretation: ״פסל לך״ — the waste shall be yours. And from where was the stone hewn? Rabbi Levi and Rabbi Hanan disagree: one said from beneath the Throne of Glory, and one said from beneath his tent was its quarry — from there he hewed two tablets for himself, and took the waste for himself, and from there Moses grew rich, for they were of sapphire. And thus it is written: ״ידיו גלילי זהב״ — these are the tablets. And how were the statements [arranged]? Five on one tablet and five on one tablet. Another interpretation: ״פסל לך״ — I am making you a king, and you may ״פוסל״ (validate or invalidate) whatever you wish." "<b>Go up to Me to the mountain and make yourself an ark of wood.</b> But Moses did not do so, as it says: ״ואעש ארון עץ״ — earlier."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "פסול here is read as both 'hew' and 'invalidate' (in the halakhic sense of פסול/כשר); the final phrase preserves the dual meaning."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:00:46.692392809Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}